  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>The bearded worm goby, Taenioides cirratus, is a mud-burrowing goby of coastal, usually estuarine waters from India to Japan and New Caledonia. It digs networks of sloping tunnels in the substrate, and has greatly reduced eyes, teeth on the outside of its mouth and the ability to breathe air and survive out of water for some time. Growing to 30 cm SL, it will eat anything small enough and is probably best kept in a species set-up.</p><ul><li>Needs a very large tank</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>India, Bangladesh, Andaman Islands, Thailand, China, Hong Kong, Japan, Taiwan, Philippines, Indonesia, New Caledonia and Papua New Guinea.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits coastal, usually estuarine waters where it lives in burrows in the muddy substrate.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Likely to be quite inactive. An aquarium of 75 cm x 12 cm x 12 cm (70 litres) should suffice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>The most important aspects would seem to be the provision of brackish (1.005-1.015 sg) water and a soft, deep substrate into which the fish can burrow. Dim lighting is advisable, seeing as this fish spends the majority of its life in the dark. Other decor does not really seem necessary.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 22–27°C. Given its natural distribution, a temperature in this range would seem advisable.</li><li>pH: 7.5–8.5</li><li>Hardness: 10–20 dGH. It's an estuarine species, so hardness in this range would seem appropriate.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Apparently feeds on crustaceans and small fish in nature, and is therefore likely to accept the usual frozen and live fare such as bloodworm, brine shrimp and chopped prawn in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Should certainly not be kept with anything small enough to be considered food. Given its weird looks and special requirements it's probably worthy of a species setup.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Unknown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Unknown and in all honesty unlikely to occur. It isn't even fully known how it reproduces in nature.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/taenioides-cirratus",
      "html": "<p>Something for the true oddball enthusiast, this bizarre looking goby has been imported at least once over the last few years. It's a mud-burrowing species that digs networks of sloping tunnels in the substrate in nature. Apparently each series of burrows has several openings, one of which has a mound outside. According to Itani and Uchino (2003), this mound suggests that the fish \"actively process sediment\". Even more odd is its appearance. It has greatly reduced eyes, specialised bronchial chambers which mean it can breathe air (allowing it to survive out of water for some time) and most crazy of all has teeth on the outside of its mouth!</p>"
    }
  ],
